.env

# Local workspace state
.DS_Store
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
.claude/settings.local.json
*.work

# Workflow run records — runtime state, not source. A halted run is durable on
# the machine that started it; committing one would replay another machine's
# position and its input fingerprints.
metis-server/.metis/runs/

# Publication baselines — the same argument. `<model>.published.json` records
# what THIS deployment last published, written only after a successful
# publication (drift.record_generation). Committing one would tell another
# machine that cases were published which never left it, and three-way drift
# would then read a real manual edit as unchanged.
*.published.json

# Joern's scratch workspace. It writes one into the working directory whenever a
# script runs, holding a copy of the CPG — binary, large, and reproducible from
# the source it was built from. `code_analysis.engine` keeps the artefacts it
# actually reuses under the target repo's .metis/cache/ instead.
workspace/

# Generated automation artefacts and anything else scratch. `metis generate
# --out tmp/testcases/<model>` writes here.
#
# **Not committed, deliberately.** A generated suite is derived from an approved
# model the same way the database is derived from `graph/` — regenerate it and
# you have it back, and committing one invites a hand-edit that the next
# regeneration silently discards. The emitters say so in every file header ("Do
# not hand-edit: regenerate after the model changes"), and a tracked copy would
# make that instruction unenforceable.
tmp/
